# L §5.14.2 Cycles: "When two decisions on one day each read what the other changes, the writer states before m or
# after m on one of them. An unordered cycle is refused ... at run, only between instances due on one day that read
# what each other changes there (RUN-ORDER)."  sweep, top and later each read konto and move money at it, on days that
# are rows (known only at run), so the three are one component of the load graph; top states `after sweep`, and
# later's day is another.  On 2026-03-10 sweep moves konto's 100.00 to save, then top, whose when reads konto's
# running balance, finds 0 and adds amt: konto 10.00, save 100.00 (carried through).  (Was RUN-ORDER "decisions sweep
# and top read each other": the walk's component dropped the written after; the probe writer's p24.  A 2.0 fault.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
place konto: accounts PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place save: accounts PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place world: outside lives 1900-01-01..
input d1: date
input d2: date
input amt: money PLN

move sweep: transfers
  on d1
  reads konto
  leg cash from konto to save amount bal(konto)

move top: transfers
  on d1
  when bal(konto) == 0
  reads konto
  after sweep
  leg cash from world to konto amount amt

move later: transfers
  on d2
  reads konto
  leg cash from konto to world amount bal(konto)

ask q: bal(konto, 2026-03-10), bal(save, 2026-03-10)

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  d1 = 2026-03-10 "own"
  d2 = 2026-06-01 "own"
  amt = 10.00 PLN "own"
  opening[konto] = 100.00 PLN at 2026-01-01 start "own"
  opening[save] = 0 PLN at 2026-01-01 start "own"
=== expect
q: (10.00 PLN, 100.00 PLN)
...
